# Overlay / Reveal System — Interaction Specification (V1)

## 1. Purpose

Overlay / Reveal is a transversal interaction system.

It is not an autonomous projection space.

Its role is to provide:

* contextual UI emergence;
* temporary local interaction surfaces;
* lightweight contextual information;
* secondary contextual actions.

Overlay / Reveal exists to avoid permanently exposing all interface complexity.

---

# 2. Conceptual Model

Reveal is defined as:

```text id="jlwm2x"
a temporary contextual UI attached to a node or local interaction target
```

Reveal is:

* local;
* temporary;
* contextual;
* secondary.

Reveal never defines the primary navigation structure.

Primary navigation remains handled by:

* Structure Space;
* Semantic Space;
* Flow Space;
* Trace Space;
* Inspection Space;
* Operational State Space.

---

# 3. Reveal Types

V1 supports lightweight contextual reveals such as:

```text id="jlwm5n"
context menus
local temporary lists
action reveals
detail reveals
preview reveals
```

Examples:

* revealing a list from a collapsed stack node;
* revealing actions for a selected semantic category;
* revealing contextual preview information;
* revealing lightweight local details.

---

# 4. Trigger Model

Reveal appearance is interaction-driven.

Supported triggers include:

```text id="jlwm7z"
click
long press
hover
ctrl + hover
selection
```

Trigger behavior depends on the active space and targeted node type.

---

# 5. Lifecycle

Reveal visibility is temporary.

V1 lifecycle behavior:

```text id="jlwm0y"
outside click closes reveal
```

Reveal also closes when:

* target disappears;
* focus changes;
* another reveal opens.

---

# 6. Exclusivity

V1 supports:

```text id="jlwm8u"
only one reveal open at a time
```

There is:

* no reveal stacking;
* no nested reveal hierarchy;
* no persistent reveal chains.

Opening a reveal closes the currently active reveal.

---

# 7. Positioning

Reveal surfaces are:

```text id="jlwm1f"
attached to the originating node
```

They are spatially contextual.

Reveal positioning follows the originating interaction target.

---

# 8. Relation With Inspector

Reveal and Inspector are distinct systems.

## Reveal

Reveal provides:

* lightweight temporary contextual information;
* quick local interaction;
* minimal contextual augmentation.

---

## Inspector

Inspector provides:

* persistent inspection;
* complete editing;
* structured detailed visualization;
* stable workspace interaction.

---

## Inspector Preview

V1 introduces:

```text id="jlwm3k"
ctrl + hover
```

to trigger lightweight inspection preview behavior without opening full persistent inspection workflows.

---

# 9. Navigation Separation

Reveal must never replace:

* native navigation;
* structural expansion;
* semantic traversal;
* persistent projection rendering.

Examples:

* semantic expand/collapse is not a reveal;
* structure navigation is not a reveal;
* flow traversal is not a reveal.

Reveal only augments existing navigation spaces.

---

# 10. Design Principles

Reveal must remain:

* lightweight;
* contextual;
* temporary;
* spatially local;
* visually non-invasive;
* secondary to navigation spaces.

The objective is:

```text id="jlwm9v"
reduce permanent interface density while preserving deep contextual interaction capability
```
